  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 PM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: This morning's 500mb analysis depicts large-scale \nlow pressure centered over Northern Ontario/Quebec with strong \nnorthern stream flow positioned over the Great Lakes Region. A \nshortwave currently over the Canadian Rockies deepens this evening \nand makes its way toward the Northwoods. At the surface, Northern \nMichigan remains benign as high pressure centered over Iowa keeps \nconditions dry this afternoon. Cloud cover builds this evening \nfrom the west as incoming low pressure tied to the \naforementioned trough approaches. Subsequent rainfall will occur\nWednesday, followed by isolated snow showers as the low \npressure continues to progress downstream.\n\nCold air advection on the backside of this cyclone will occupy the \nregion this weekend, with highs staying in the upper 30s to low 40s \nthrough Sunday. Long range ensembles show midlevel heights rising as \nthe CWA becomes cut off from northern stream flow. Midlevel ridging \nwill support high pressure through the rest of the forecast period \nwith temperatures slowly building back into the 50s as we head \ninto next week.\n\n\nForecast Details: \n\nTonight through Thursday: Pleasant weather will continue this \nafternoon through tonight as high pressure moves to our southwest. \nCurrent sunny skies will slowly be replaced by cloud cover building \nfrom the west. Light, disheveled rainfall will be observed Wednesday \nmorning as surface low pressure progresses across the northern \nOhio Valley. Even with deeper saturation to our south, enough \nlow level moisture and lift associated with a weak cold front \nwill produce around a tenth inch or so QPF through Wednesday. \n\nMore noticeable weather is in store for Thursday as cold cP air \ndraws in from the north and begins to switch rainfall to snow \nshowers. Unlike Wednesday\u2019s widespread rain chances, snow \nshowers will be confined to cooler thermal profiles of the CWA. \nWhile rain and snow showers are expected across typical North to\nNNE zones overnight Wednesday through Thursday, the best areas \nof convergence expected to have snow stick to the ground will be\nsouth and west of Grand Traverse Bay, especially elevated \nterrain. Because surface temperatures remain near freezing and \nsoil's are still warm from the abnormal fall season, areas that\ndo observe accumulating snowfall are expected to remain less \nthan an inch at this time. Lake effect rain with snow mixing in \nwill continue through the day Thursday, but remain more cellular\nas diurnal disruptions over land limit horizontal roll \nconvection.\n\nFriday through Monday Outlook: Backside northerly flow continues \nthrough Thursday night into Friday affecting the same northern flow \nlocations. Enhancement overnight with strong convergence over Grand \nTraverse Bay will continue to deliver highest intensity and \naccumulations to elevated terrain south of TVC and northern Wexford \nCounty. Highest probabilities of snow accumulations remain below an \ninch at this time, but if any bands can materialize with cold \nenough overnight lows, locally higher amounts could occur. \n\nSubsidence building over the Great Lakes Region Friday \nafternoon/Saturday will dry conditions across the CWA and return\nhigh pressure through the remainder of the forecast period. \nQuiet and benign weather is supported with sunny skies and highs\naround 40. Temperatures trend warmer again early next week as \nnortherly flow moves north of the Great Lakes region and surface\nwinds turn south west. Widespread highs in the 50s can be \nexpected as we head toward thanksgiving.",
